00:03:45.379 --> 00:03:46.680
This  is  a  story  about  AI.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:55.719
And  it  begins  with  the  one  phrase
that  Wikipedians  all  over  the  world

00:03:55.719 --> 00:03:59.460
have  been  saying  whenever  being
asked  about  the  quality  of  Wikipedia

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:03.539
and  saying,  Wikipedia  might  be
a  good  place  to  start  research.

00:04:03.539 --> 00:04:05.800
It's  definitely  a  terrible
place  to  end  research.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:11.219
And  if  we  truly  believe  this  statement,
then  there  is  a  moral  obligation

00:04:11.219 --> 00:04:17.660
of  Wikipedians  to  provide  resources
to  continue  research  somewhere  else.

00:04:18.259 --> 00:04:22.699
And  one  of  these  tools  is
providing  literature  and  references.

00:04:22.699 --> 00:04:29.040
And  these  references  in  Wikipedia  are
important.  They  are  an  integral  part  of  it.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:34.139
And  we  love  any  means
possible  to  make  access  to

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:37.620
these  materials  that  we
reference  easy  and  possible.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:41.639
So,  if  you  just  mention
an  ISBN  in  Media Wiki,  it

00:04:41.639 --> 00:04:45.300
will  automatically  be
converted  into  a  special  link.

00:04:45.439 --> 00:04:49.660
And  the  special  link  will  take
you  to  national  libraries  and  to

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:55.620
bookstores  and  to  smaller  libraries
and  to  second -hand  places.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:03.279
So,  any  place  you  could  find  this  literature
should  be  mentioned  there,  one  way  or  another.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:13.759
And  the  ISBNs...  ISBNs  in  general
have  lots  of  cool  features.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.160
There  is  a  hierarchy  in  there.

00:05:16.379 --> 00:05:23.500
So,  the  first  character  of  the  ISBN  is
going  to  tell  you  about  the  language.

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:31.620
And  there  are  different  categories  of
parts  that  relate  to  the  publisher.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:34.480
But  the  last  digit  is  a  checksum.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:40.819
There  are  two  different  formulas  for
calculating  the  ISBN  10  and  ISBN  13  checksums.

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:47.259
But  they  all  boil  down  to  basically  the
sum  of  the  product  of  different  digits.

00:05:47.259 --> 00:05:53.399
And  there  is  a  formula  and  there
is  a  mathematical  proof  that  it  is

00:05:53.399 --> 00:06:01.100
impossible  to  switch  to  numbers  without
resulting  in  a  mismatched  checksum.

00:06:01.279 --> 00:06:06.779
So,  if  you  use  an  ISBN,  there
is  a  good  chance  that  you

00:06:06.779 --> 00:06:11.379
will  be  able  to  verify  that
the  ISBN  is  indeed  a  valid  one.

00:06:13.459 --> 00:06:20.759
And  as  part  of  me  playing  around  with
technology,  I  wrote  a  checksum  checker.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:27.180
So,  I  downloaded  the  whole  content
from  the  German  language  Wikipedia.

00:06:27.959 --> 00:06:32.959
I  wrote  a  small  parser  looking
for  ISBNs  in  various  places.

00:06:32.959 --> 00:06:34.180
Some  of  them  are  in  templates.

00:06:34.399 --> 00:06:38.180
Some  of  them  are  just
mentioned  in  the  article  itself.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.939
I  extracted  the  ISBN  and
then  I  ran  the  checksum

00:06:42.939 --> 00:06:46.220
algorithm  to  see  if
there  are  any  mismatches.

00:06:46.879 --> 00:06:50.620
This  is  pretty  straightforward.  This
is  not  rocket  science  by  any  means.

00:06:50.759 --> 00:06:57.839
This  is  just  parsing,  in  the  case  of  German
language  would  be  30  gigabytes  of  text  files.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:05.019
It's  something  that  should
have  been  done  earlier.

00:07:05.379 --> 00:07:07.779
And  in  some  cases,  it  might  have
been  done  already  by  someone  else.

00:07:07.899 --> 00:07:09.980
But  I  wasn't  aware  of
this  work  being  done.

00:07:10.399 --> 00:07:17.399
And  I  started  this  work  with  two  use
cases  of  things  I  was  hoping  to  find.

00:07:17.399 --> 00:07:28.699
The  first  one  is  ISBN  references  with  mismatched
ISBNs  because  of  any  kind  of  human  mistake.

00:07:29.019 --> 00:07:33.560
So,  the  idea  was  to  find  ISBNs
where  people  had  inadvertently

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:39.740
switched  different  numbers
or  where  a  one  had  turned

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:44.060
into  a  two  or  a  zero  into  a
nine  or  something  like  this.

00:07:44.399 --> 00:07:45.720
Any  kind  of  honest  mistake.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:52.600
And  the  second  category  is  a  bit
funny  because  in  the  80s  and  90s,  many

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:55.699
publishers  were  completely  unable  to
understand  the  concept  of  checksums.

00:07:55.899 --> 00:07:59.420
So,  they  usually  just
incremented  their  ISBNs  by  one.

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:03.360
So,  the  next  volume  just
became  plus  one  and  so  on.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:10.220
And  libraries  all  over  the  world  are
capable  of  handling  these  situations.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:13.879
So,  in  most  library  catalogues,
there  is  a  special  field  for  saying,

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:17.839
well,  this  ISBN  is  technically
wrong,  but  it's  printed  on  the  book.

00:08:18.019 --> 00:08:23.720
And  if  it  works  like  an  ISBN  and  it
works,  then  we're  just  going  to  accept  this.

00:08:23.879 --> 00:08:28.759
So,  if  you  come  across  these,
usually  from  the  80s,  90s,  wrong

00:08:28.759 --> 00:08:32.279
ISBNs,  they  are  still  going  to  work
in  library  catalogues  and  so  on.

00:08:32.279 --> 00:08:35.240
Sometimes  you  will  get  an
angry  remark  saying,  this

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:38.100
is  a  wrong  ISBN,  but  it
still  works  as  an  ISBN.

00:08:38.279 --> 00:08:44.340
And  Wikipedia  has  a  template,  in  the
German  language,  it's  called  ISBN  falsch,

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:52.639
simply  saying,  yes,  technically  the  ISBN
is  wrong,  but  the  link  is  going  to  work

00:08:52.639 --> 00:08:57.639
and  nothing  is  happening  preventing
you  from  accessing  the  content

00:08:57.639 --> 00:09:01.059
or  finding  the  library
in  question  that  has  the

00:09:01.059 --> 00:09:06.940
book  or  any  kind  of  physical
or  non -corporal  medium

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:08.940
that  contains  the  information
you're  looking  for.

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:14.929
What  I  did  find  were
three  categories  of  errors.

00:09:15.389 --> 00:09:17.889
The  first  one,  I  found  a  couple
of  them.  I  corrected  them.

00:09:19.289 --> 00:09:22.570
I  found  the  second  category  of  errors.

00:09:23.309 --> 00:09:28.309
And  I  could  simply  add  the  template  to
indicate,  yes,  it's  technically  a  wrong  ISBN,

00:09:28.509 --> 00:09:32.370
but  it's  still  going  to  work  like  one
because  everybody  else  is  aware  of  the  error.

00:09:33.029 --> 00:09:38.470
And  then  I  found  a  third  category  that  I
wasn't  aware  of  and  I  hadn't  anticipated.

00:09:38.830 --> 00:09:41.049
And  this  gave  me  quite  a  headache.

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:46.730
So  I  found  articles  in
Wikipedia  that  contained  two,

00:09:46.889 --> 00:09:52.049
sometimes  small,  literature
references  containing  references

00:09:52.049 --> 00:09:53.970
that  had  checksum  errors.

00:09:54.350 --> 00:10:00.490
And  the  book  titles  and  the  author  names,
they  all  sounded  plausible,  familiar,

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:05.809
and  sometimes  they  were  indeed  the  names  of
authors  that  are  published  in  this  field.

00:10:06.009 --> 00:10:10.450
But  I  couldn't,  for  the  life  of  me,
couldn't  find  the  literature  in  any  catalogue.

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:17.389
I  could  find  similar  literature,  I
could  find  things  of  similar  titles,

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:19.809
but  I  couldn't  find  the  literature  itself.

00:10:20.049 --> 00:10:25.070
It's  always  something  that  was  missing  or
anything  like  this.  And  then  it  hit  me.

00:10:26.009 --> 00:10:31.029
And  you  probably  know  how
this  is  going  to  be  resolved.

00:10:32.809 --> 00:10:39.330
These  literature  references  were
hallucinated  by,  in  most  cases,  by  ChatGPT.

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:46.669
So  people  had  asked  ChatGPT
to  write  Wikipedia  articles,

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:54.210
and  in  their  compliance  and  in
their  training  data  as  trained,

00:10:54.789 --> 00:10:59.289
the  large  language  model  simply
provided  both  the  article

00:10:59.289 --> 00:11:02.509
and  a  set  of  completely
hallucinated  literature  references,

00:11:02.509 --> 00:11:05.649
and  people  had  simply  cut  and
pasted  this  into  Wikipedia.

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:18.409
ChatGPT  did  what  it  was  asked  to
do,  and  people  thought  what  they

00:11:18.409 --> 00:11:23.269
did  was  the  logical  next  step  of
dumping  the  content  into  Wikipedia.

00:11:26.409 --> 00:11:29.429
Wikipedia  is  not  alone  in  this  discovery.

00:11:31.029 --> 00:11:38.169
So  the  University  Library  of
Hagen,  already  in  2023,  reported

00:11:38.169 --> 00:11:41.950
about  students  asking  the
librarians  to  find  literature

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:47.730
references  that  matched  existing
journals,  existing  issues,

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:52.649
but  the  other  information  was
simply  unable  to  be  verified.

00:11:53.509 --> 00:11:59.450
The  International  Committee
of  the  Red  Cross  has  issued

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:03.350
a  strongly  worded  warning
on  hallucinated  references,

00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:07.610
because  the  next  thing  they  encountered
when  telling  people  that  the

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:11.210
literature  or  the  references  they
are  looking  for  does  not  exist,

00:12:11.389 --> 00:12:14.110
is  they  are  being  accused
of  hiding  the  truth.

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:27.169
So  this  is  a  much  more  unpleasant  reaction
than  the  ones  I  was  aware  of  until  then.

00:12:27.549 --> 00:12:33.710
In  most  cases,  it's  simply
a  waste  of  time  of  staff  at

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:37.009
libraries  hunting  down
literature  that  doesn't  exist.

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:43.870
There's  a  beautiful  article  about
the  situation  in  404  Media.

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:45.789
I  can  highly  recommend  this.

00:12:46.149 --> 00:12:52.830
And  I  forgot  to  include  the  link,  so  I
failed  on  the  providing  references  part.

00:12:54.919 --> 00:12:57.899
And  this  is  the  part
where  this  turns  from  a

00:12:57.899 --> 00:13:01.480
story  about  AI  into  a
story  about  AI  and  humans.

00:13:03.019 --> 00:13:06.899
And  I  would  like  to
remind  people  that  this  is,

00:13:07.179 --> 00:13:10.519
as  such,  not  a  new  issue
we  are  dealing  with.

00:13:10.659 --> 00:13:16.980
So  this  is  an  old  XKCD  comic
making  fun  or  describing  a

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:20.639
scenario  in  which  circular
references  are  being  created.

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:25.899
Someone  makes  up  something,
puts  it  in  Wikipedia.

00:13:27.159 --> 00:13:33.659
Someone  else  is  copying  the  information,
putting  the  stuff  into  a  newspaper  article.

00:13:34.139 --> 00:13:38.259
Someone  else  is  saying,  oh,  there  is  a
citation  missing  in  the  Wikipedia  article,

00:13:38.419 --> 00:13:45.480
and  by  this  time  there  is  sufficient
information  online  supposedly  verifying  the  claim.

00:13:45.679 --> 00:13:51.940
So  the  circle  of  reference  is
established  and  it's  getting  stronger.

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:56.179
There  are  hilarious  examples
in  which  the  German

00:13:56.179 --> 00:14:00.159
president,  not  the  current
one  but  an  older  one,

00:14:01.659 --> 00:14:10.500
gave  a  Laudatio  to  the  German  national
soccer  team,  the  female  soccer  team,

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:21.200
praising  progress  in  gender  equality
and  making  remarks  on  how  former

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:28.100
teams  were  awarded  dishes  as  a
present  when  winning  championships.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:37.879
And  this  was  half  true,  some  part  of  it  was
true,  the  other  one  didn't  turn  out  to  be  true,

00:14:38.139 --> 00:14:40.360
but  now  it's  a  circular
reference  because  people  are

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:43.720
referencing  the  German  president
for  making  this  claim.

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:49.519
And  it's  increasingly  hard
to  disprove  the  non -existence

00:14:49.519 --> 00:14:53.419
of  a  fact  when  there  is  plenty
of  evidence  to  cite  from.

00:14:55.480 --> 00:15:00.600
So  the  best  way  to  stop
such  a  circular  reference

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:05.740
is  to  prevent  it  from
ever  getting  it  started.

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:12.639
And  yes,  there  is  a  bigger
issue  at  stake  and  it's

00:15:12.639 --> 00:15:17.620
not  just  the  one  article
that  is  now  contaminated.

00:15:18.879 --> 00:15:25.360
It  is,  I  think,  a  good  example  on  the
shortcomings  of  existing  large  language  models.

00:15:25.639 --> 00:15:30.539
And  you  might  have  seen
articles  discussing  whether  this

00:15:30.539 --> 00:15:33.419
is  a  systemic  issue  that
can  never  be  fully  resolved,

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:39.100
that  large  language  models
are  machines  designed  to

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:46.240
provide  plausible  tokens
following  a  certain  sequence.

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:51.659
So  reality  or  factuality  is  never
an  issue,  it's  about  plausibility.

00:15:52.240 --> 00:15:57.320
And  this  is  what  one  could  describe
as  a  kind  of  anti -knowledge.

00:15:57.980 --> 00:16:03.399
The  anti -thesis  to  what  an  encyclopedia
project  like  Wikipedia  is  about.

00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:10.659
And  also  there  is  quite
a  deep  irony  in  there.

00:16:12.139 --> 00:16:18.740
Wikipedia  has  proven  to  be  one
of  the  most  prominent  source

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:23.559
of  training  data  for  most
of  the  large  language  models.

00:16:23.879 --> 00:16:27.460
So  these  model  providers
are  using  Wikipedia.

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:29.440
They  are  using  tons  of
other  information  too.

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:33.419
But  Wikipedia  content  is  useful
because  it's  multilingual.

00:16:33.779 --> 00:16:36.259
It  has  a  rich  density  of  information.

00:16:37.019 --> 00:16:41.220
There  is  a  network  of  information
and  links  between.  It's  multilingual.

00:16:41.779 --> 00:16:46.500
In  so  many  ways  there  is  a  version  history
providing  kind  of  a  temporal  concept.

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:56.379
So  the  large  language  model
providers  are  in  some  way  poisoning

00:16:56.379 --> 00:17:00.340
their  own  pond  from  which  they
are  drinking  the  water  from.

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:04.759
And  there  is,  I  was,  I
gave  a  similar  speech  in

00:17:04.759 --> 00:17:07.200
the  conference  of  Wikipedia
a  couple  of  months  ago.

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:11.359
And  I  was  told,  as  an  offhand
remark  by  someone,  these

00:17:11.359 --> 00:17:17.200
model  providers  are  approaching
organizations  for  content.

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:20.839
They  are  still  in  the  acquisition
phase  of  asking  for  more  content.

00:17:21.079 --> 00:17:27.960
And  they  are  asking  for  content  that  is
proven  to  be  free  from  synthetic  information.

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:33.400
So  they  are  quite  aware  of  the
issue  and  they  are  paying  a  premium

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:37.480
for  getting  content  that  is  free
from  their  own  contaminations.

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:44.700
If  there  is  an  irony  in  there,  I
may  be  by  now  too  angry  to  see  it.

00:17:45.619 --> 00:17:54.430
But  your  mileage  may  vary.  You  might  still
see  the  irony  here.  Back  to  Wikipedia.

00:17:54.890 --> 00:17:57.089
Is  there  a  consensus  how
to  deal  with  the  content?

00:17:57.089 --> 00:18:02.250
And  as  far  as  I  can
tell,  and  this  is  something

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:06.789
that  is  always  kind  of
a  status  quo  discussion.

00:18:07.589 --> 00:18:11.349
There  seems  to  be  a  consensus
building  up  saying  that  at  least

00:18:11.349 --> 00:18:17.910
the  undeclared  use  of  large
language  model  is  frowned  upon.

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:23.309
And  people  have  been  banned
from  Wikipedia  for  dumping

00:18:23.309 --> 00:18:27.049
large  language  model  created
content  into  Wikipedia.

00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:31.049
It's  a  kind  of  vandalism
that  we  have  seen  before.

00:18:31.869 --> 00:18:36.849
We  have  dealt  with  this
because  we  were  concerned  about

00:18:36.849 --> 00:18:40.470
copyright  issues,  about  people
doing  all  kinds  of  things.

00:18:40.470 --> 00:18:46.190
But  in  this  case,  there  is  some
sort  of  consensus  building  up.

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:50.069
Other  languages  are  falling  to  some  degree.

00:18:50.269 --> 00:18:53.049
There  is  a  new  speedy
deletion  rule  in  the  English

00:18:53.049 --> 00:18:57.589
language  Wikipedia  that  has
a  slightly  higher  threshold.

00:18:57.589 --> 00:19:06.210
And  saying,  okay,  this  is  about  blatantly
obvious  content  identified  as  LLM  generated.

00:19:06.809 --> 00:19:11.549
I  think  this  is  a  good  first
step  in  the  right  direction.

00:19:11.710 --> 00:19:17.009
But  at  the  same  time,  the  bar
keeps  getting  graced  by  the  changes

00:19:17.009 --> 00:19:20.690
in  how  the  models  work  and  the
kind  of  content  they  are  producing.

00:19:23.690 --> 00:19:32.490
So,  does  the  policy  work?  Also  hard  to
tell.  Maybe  to  some  degree  it  does  work.

00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:39.930
But  in  general,  there  is  too  much
unknown  at  this  point  because  this

00:19:39.930 --> 00:19:46.930
scenario  I  described  of  content
being  identified  as  LLM  generated

00:19:46.930 --> 00:19:50.349
because  two  or  more  ISBN
references  mismatched.

00:19:50.569 --> 00:19:52.789
This  is  a  highly  specific  scenario.

00:19:53.309 --> 00:20:00.289
And  most  of  the  cases  where  content  has
been  dumped  into  Wikipedia  has  other  issues.

00:20:00.609 --> 00:20:06.950
And  there  are  other  telltale  signs  that
help  Wikipedians  identify  this  content.

00:20:06.950 --> 00:20:10.970
At  a  conference  in  Potsdam  a
couple  of  months  ago,  I  was  told

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:15.210
that  most  current  large  language
models  are  completely  unable  to

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:20.210
consistently  produce  the  kind  of
MediaW iki  syntax  that  Wikipedia  uses.

00:20:20.210 --> 00:20:23.589
They  are  still  falling
back  to  Markdown  language.

00:20:23.809 --> 00:20:28.150
Sometimes  they  are  using
the  English  language

00:20:28.150 --> 00:20:31.329
template  names  rather  than
the  German  template  names.

00:20:31.329 --> 00:20:34.769
And  then  they  sometimes  translate  the
English  template  names  into  German.

00:20:34.930 --> 00:20:38.809
So,  this  might  result  in  the
usage  of  non -existent  templates.

00:20:39.190 --> 00:20:43.289
Sometimes  they  add  parameters  into
the  templates  that  do  not  exist.

00:20:43.549 --> 00:20:47.930
So,  these  are  things  that  a  slightly
skilled  Wikipedian  is  able  to  detect.

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:53.410
But  there  is  no  fixed
set  of  small  rules  that

00:20:53.410 --> 00:20:56.470
helps  people  to  identify
LLM  generated  content.

00:20:56.470 --> 00:20:59.569
It's  always  kind  of  a  one
screw  up  and  then  kind

00:20:59.569 --> 00:21:03.930
of  a  backtracing  the
actions  of  certain  users.

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:11.569
And  this  is  the  next  part  is  the  why.

00:21:11.970 --> 00:21:20.609
So,  the  first  part  is  the  what  the  fuck  part
and  the  other  one  is  the  why  the  fuck  part.

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:26.589
I'm  going  to  present  four
different  theories  on  why

00:21:26.589 --> 00:21:29.609
people  are  doing  this  and
these  might  not  be  conclusive.

00:21:30.609 --> 00:21:33.670
And  I'm  refraining  from
saying  it  has  to  be  one

00:21:33.670 --> 00:21:36.170
of  these  four,  but  I'm
open  to  new  suggestions.

00:21:36.670 --> 00:21:42.269
The  first  one  is  the  explanation  stemming  from
the " assume  good  faith"  rule  in  Wikipedia.

00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:48.349
Some  people  might  simply  not
know  what  the  capabilities

00:21:48.349 --> 00:21:50.769
and  limitations  of  large
language  models  are.

00:21:50.990 --> 00:21:56.150
And  they  are  in  some
state  of  blissful  ignorance

00:21:56.150 --> 00:21:57.950
thinking  that  they  are
doing  something  good.

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:03.130
So,  they  might  consider  their
dumping  of  content  generated

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:06.549
by  LLMs  as  kind  of  a  service
to  the  whole  community  saying,

00:22:06.549 --> 00:22:12.829
yes,  we  added  a  new  article.  It  looks
like  knowledge.  What's  wrong  with  that?

00:22:15.170 --> 00:22:21.650
I  have  yet  to  find  a  person
qualifying  for  this  kind  of  explanation.

00:22:21.650 --> 00:22:26.150
I  would  like  to  see  one
someday.  The  people  I  encountered

00:22:26.150 --> 00:22:31.990
with,  the  people  I  talked  to,
do  not  fall  into  this  category.

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:36.910
Then  there  is  one,  and
this  is  the,  so  we  are

00:22:36.910 --> 00:22:40.910
moving  into  darker  and
more  frustrating  territory.

00:22:43.250 --> 00:22:48.750
It's  people  being  aware  of  their
inability  to  contribute  to  Wikipedia  in

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:53.950
a  meaningful  manner  and  trying  to
compensate  this  by  getting  outside  help.

00:22:55.650 --> 00:23:00.509
I'm  trying  neutral  description,  but  it  might
not,  I  might  have  failed  on  this  one.

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:10.470
So,  in  theory,  this  could  at
least  be  kind  of  a  non -evil,  well

00:23:10.470 --> 00:23:15.829
-meaning  thing  of  saying,  okay,  I'm
dyslexic,  I  need  a  spell  checker.

00:23:16.609 --> 00:23:20.750
I  can't  do  proper  research,  so
I  need  an  assistant  to  do  so.

00:23:20.849 --> 00:23:26.089
It  might  be  some  sort  of  equivalent.
I'm  happy  to  meet  these  people  too.

00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:34.049
Then  there  is  the  agenda  part,  to
which  I'm  happily  going  to  refer  to  a

00:23:34.049 --> 00:23:39.869
presentation  made  from  Asaf  Bartow  at
the  Wikimedia  Conference  this  year,

00:23:40.049 --> 00:23:45.869
where  he  described  the  weaponization
of  LLMs,  of  saying,  okay,  let's  change

00:23:45.869 --> 00:23:51.710
10,  100,  1000  articles  through  the
use  of  one,  just  one  motivated  person,

00:23:51.710 --> 00:23:59.490
rewriting  history,  rewriting  this.
He  had  fun  exploring  the  limitations

00:23:59.490 --> 00:24:04.150
of  LLMs  and  the  safeguards  and  LLMs
saying,  I'm  not  going  to  do  this.

00:24:04.509 --> 00:24:10.250
I  also  had  fun  during  his  presentation
jailbreaking  these  safeguards  and

00:24:10.250 --> 00:24:15.329
talking  large  language  models  into
collaborating  with  me  to  rewrite  history.

00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:19.750
And  there's  always  the  fourth
one,  it  might  be  something  else.

00:24:21.490 --> 00:24:25.789
So,  this  is  the  catch -all  explanation,
it  might  be  something  else.

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:33.630
But  then  again,  this  is  an  open
invitation  for  you  to  add  your  experiences.

00:24:35.950 --> 00:24:40.730
In  most  cases  where  I
discovered  content  written  by

00:24:40.730 --> 00:24:43.230
large  language  models  and
dumped  into  it  from  users,

00:24:43.829 --> 00:24:48.289
I  tried  to  contact  these  users,  I  tried
to  get  them  to  confirm  the  usage  of  LLMs.

00:24:49.809 --> 00:24:57.170
And  there  was  one  question  I  asked  them
consistently  is,  please  show  me  the  prompt.

00:24:57.529 --> 00:25:01.410
I  asked  them  to  tell  me,  could  you
please  show  me  the  prompt  that  you  used?

00:25:01.630 --> 00:25:05.109
And  this  is  now  my  kind
of  litmus  test  of  saying,

00:25:05.309 --> 00:25:10.430
if  you  are  climbing
into  the  ignorance  part,

00:25:10.430 --> 00:25:15.750
saying,  oh,  I  just  used  this  as  a  tool
to  copy  edit  the  work  I  already  did,

00:25:16.089 --> 00:25:19.890
it  shouldn't  be  a  problem  for
you  to  give  me  the  prompt.

00:25:20.529 --> 00:25:24.710
Most  of  the  large  language  model
providers  have  share  functions,

00:25:24.950 --> 00:25:28.289
allowing  you  to  completely  share
the  conversation  if  you  like.

00:25:28.490 --> 00:25:32.789
And  the  amount  of  excuses  I  got  is
impressive  in  terms  of  creativity.

00:25:32.789 --> 00:25:44.950
So,  I  was  never  logged  in,  the  dog  ate  my
prompt,  I  cancelled  this,  I  can't  find  it  anymore,

00:25:46.849 --> 00:25:50.470
oh,  how  dare  you  ask  me  these
questions,  these  are  sensitive  information.

00:25:53.549 --> 00:26:01.789
This  is  not  a  proof  that
is  made  to  be  foolproof,

00:26:01.789 --> 00:26:07.690
in  a  sense,  but  this  is  my  personal  litmus
test  of  testing  whether  people  are  sincere

00:26:07.690 --> 00:26:11.490
and  whether  their  ignorance  is  plausible.

00:26:12.589 --> 00:26:21.009
And  this  might  be  the  reason  why  I  might  sound
slightly  more  frustrated  than  I  usually  are

00:26:21.009 --> 00:26:25.049
when  it  comes  to  technology,  because  I  like
technology  and  I  like  to  play  with  this.

00:26:25.210 --> 00:26:31.089
And  I'm  still  hoping  that  things  are  getting
better  through  some  sense  of  useful  technology.

00:26:34.019 --> 00:26:36.460
So,  this  is  part  three,
this  is  about  humans.

00:26:41.410 --> 00:26:44.269
Remember  the  slide  when  I  said
I  wrote  the  checksum  checker?

00:26:44.349 --> 00:26:45.309
No,  I  didn't  write  it.

00:26:48.410 --> 00:26:52.210
I  theoretically  know  how  to  write
it,  but  I'm  not  good  at  it,

00:26:52.390 --> 00:26:54.170
and  I'm  getting  easily  frustrated.

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:58.809
So,  the  checksum  checker  was
written  through  the  use  of  an  AI.

00:26:58.809 --> 00:27:02.109
In  this  case,  it's  Claude  Opus.

00:27:03.009 --> 00:27:08.829
I  had  so  much  fun  talking  to  a  large
language  model,  providing  me  with  Python

00:27:08.829 --> 00:27:14.369
and  later  with  a  C  code
to  parse  Wikipedia  content.

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:22.730
The  source  code  that  was  the  result  of
this  conversation  is  released  on  GitHub.

00:27:22.730 --> 00:27:28.069
It  is  openly  declared  to  be  a
result  of  large  language  model  use,

00:27:28.289 --> 00:27:34.890
and  I  personally  think  that  this  is  an
acceptable  use  of  large  language  models.

00:27:37.269 --> 00:27:46.009
But  I'm  also  open  to  criticism,  and  there
are  plenty  of  avenues  how  to  base  this  on.

00:27:49.349 --> 00:27:59.509
At  this  point,  it's  not  a  bold  claim  to  say
that  AI  might  not  be  going  away  anytime  soon.

00:28:00.730 --> 00:28:04.910
Some  companies  might  be  going  away,  some
business  models  might  be  going  away,

00:28:04.910 --> 00:28:10.829
but  in  general,  the  whole  concept  of
transformer -based  large  language  models

00:28:10.829 --> 00:28:14.829
is  in  some  way,  shape
or  form  going  to  stay.

00:28:15.269 --> 00:28:22.869
And  I'm  slightly  concerned
about  Wikipedia  as  a  project,

00:28:22.869 --> 00:28:33.130
but  also  the  impression  that  many  people  are
simply  completely  unaware  of  the  limitations,

00:28:33.130 --> 00:28:40.910
and  the  lack  of  responsibility  of  companies
properly  explaining  these  limitations.

00:28:43.529 --> 00:28:50.569
The  ISBN  checker  is  unlikely  to
stay  a  tool  to  detect  this  content,

00:28:50.670 --> 00:28:54.869
because  I  strongly  believe  that  at
some  point  the  large  language  model

00:28:54.869 --> 00:28:57.329
will  be  able  to
hallucinate  proper  checksums,

00:28:57.329 --> 00:29:07.730
and  even  in  best -case  scenario  we  are  at  a  1
in  10  or  a  1  in  11  chance  of  getting  it  right.

00:29:07.829 --> 00:29:11.369
I  believe  at  this  point  the
chances  are  already  slimmer,

00:29:11.430 --> 00:29:16.730
because  there  is  a  pattern
in  this  generation  of  numbers.

00:29:20.029 --> 00:29:22.569
And  this  is  why  I  say
this  is  about  humans.

00:29:24.369 --> 00:29:32.000
The  AI  is  never,  and  this  is,
IBM  said  this  in  the  70s  or  60s,

00:29:32.400 --> 00:29:35.259
AI  is  never  going  to  be
able  to  assume  responsibility.

00:29:35.660 --> 00:29:38.339
This  is  always  the
responsibility  of  a  human.

00:29:40.039 --> 00:29:44.279
And  the  first  part  has  to  be
some  sort  of  conversation  about

00:29:44.279 --> 00:29:48.740
who's  taking  responsibility
for  the  use  of  tools,

00:29:49.119 --> 00:29:54.700
and  what  are  the  conditions  in  which
this  use  is  accepted  by  a  community,

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:56.440
a  society,  someone  else.

00:29:57.920 --> 00:30:01.059
So  the  minimum  requirement
I  was  able  to  identify

00:30:01.059 --> 00:30:03.680
is  the  open  declaration
of  the  usage  of  LLM.

00:30:03.680 --> 00:30:08.019
And  after  I  gave  a
similar  talk  in  Nairobi,

00:30:08.420 --> 00:30:13.480
I  was  approached  by  researchers
who  are  working  exactly  on  that

00:30:13.480 --> 00:30:15.960
and  trying  to  write  proposals.

00:30:16.339 --> 00:30:25.200
And  the  part  that  makes  me  sleep
slightly  better  than  I  should  be  able  to

00:30:25.200 --> 00:30:28.420
is  the  fact  that  the
feedback  I  got  from  humans

00:30:28.420 --> 00:30:31.420
is  overwhelmingly  constructive  and  positive.

00:30:31.420 --> 00:30:35.619
So  the  first  thing  engineers
from  Wikimedia  Foundation  did

00:30:35.619 --> 00:30:38.400
is  they  tried  to  recreate  the  numbers

00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:41.319
and  check  whether  other
languages  are  affected  too,

00:30:41.579 --> 00:30:43.500
what  the  percentage  of  these  issues  are,

00:30:43.619 --> 00:30:46.279
and  yes,  other  language
editions  are  affected  too.

00:30:48.140 --> 00:30:54.279
The  small  checksum  tool  I  did  contributed
to  the  German  language  Wikipedia

00:30:54.279 --> 00:31:01.460
being  the  only  Wikipedia  edition
had  to  drop  in  mismatches  in  ISBNs.

00:31:01.839 --> 00:31:08.279
So  it  is  possible  at  a  human  scale  to
significantly  influence  the  quality  of  an  issue

00:31:08.279 --> 00:31:10.539
or  the  quality  of
Wikipedia  in  certain  areas.

00:31:11.259 --> 00:31:14.440
I'm  happy  that  other  people  in  other
language  editions  are  helping  too,

00:31:15.220 --> 00:31:17.460
but  it's  definitely  not
enough  at  this  point.

00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:25.400
So  this  is  the  part  where  it  becomes
speculative  and  kind  of  a  plea  for  help

00:31:25.400 --> 00:31:28.220
and  saying  this  is  not
something  I  can  do  myself.

00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:30.700
This  is  something  I
can  point  to  the  issue.

00:31:30.859 --> 00:31:36.420
I  can  say,  how  do  we  communicate
the  issues  and  limitations?

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:43.160
How  do  we,  how  can  we  look  for
tools  that  can  mitigate  the  issue?

00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:48.720
Is  there,  aside  from  the  technology,  is
there  a  legal,  a  political,  a  moral  pathway

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:54.579
to  shame  companies  into
improving  their  tools?

00:31:54.579 --> 00:32:03.259
So,  for  example,  ChatGPT  saying  in  a
slightly  light  grey  on  dark  grey  colour,

00:32:03.539 --> 00:32:06.720
oh,  the  results  might  be
inaccurate.  Is  this  enough?

00:32:07.819 --> 00:32:13.799
Is  the  European  Union  going  to  help  us  in
the  application  of  the  AI  Act,  for  example?

00:32:14.099 --> 00:32:17.259
This  is  the  conversation  I'm  taking
part  in,  and  you  should  too.

00:32:18.940 --> 00:32:24.279
And  the  last  one  is  the  kind
of  a  one -size -fits -all  question.

00:32:24.440 --> 00:32:28.220
So,  is  there  still  a  place  for
an  encyclopedia  in  this  world?

00:32:32.660 --> 00:32:40.000
And  this  is  a  question  I'm  asking  completely
unrelated  to  other  people  in  other  countries

00:32:41.220 --> 00:32:54.599
releasing  LLM  rewrites  of  Wikipedia
or  things  that  have  different  agendas.

00:32:57.640 --> 00:33:03.160
So,  the  part  I  really  liked  is  the
aftermath  of  me  pointing  out  the  issues,

00:33:03.480 --> 00:33:10.279
the  feedback  I  got,  the  constructive,
helpful,  slightly  concerned  criticism

00:33:10.279 --> 00:33:15.819
on  what  should  be  done
next,  how  to  improve  this.

00:33:18.440 --> 00:33:23.619
My  pe rsonal  to -do,  and  this  is
unchanged,  is  I  would  like  to  integrate

00:33:25.359 --> 00:33:28.700
actual  bibliographic  databases
into  this  check  to  see,  okay,

00:33:29.680 --> 00:33:35.460
is  the  title  matching  to  some  degree,  are
offered  names  matching  years,  publisher  names?

00:33:36.220 --> 00:33:39.960
Are  there  any  available  reliable
data  sources  from  national  libraries

00:33:39.960 --> 00:33:44.900
that  could  help  me  find  more  hallucinated
content  that  happens  to  have  a  correct  ISBN

00:33:44.900 --> 00:33:50.599
or  doesn't  have  an  ISBN  at  all?  And
also,  this  is  a  plea  from  my  side.

00:33:50.799 --> 00:33:56.240
If  you  are  a  librarian,  if  you
want  to  become  a  librarian,  join.

00:33:57.240 --> 00:34:00.700
I  published  the  source
code,  which  was,  as  I  said,

00:34:02.039 --> 00:34:04.819
to  a  large  degree  LLM
generated  at  these  places.

00:34:05.039 --> 00:34:10.659
I'm  also  doing  a  rewrite  into  a  more  flexible
tool  that  does  a  couple  of  other  tests  too.

00:34:12.619 --> 00:34:19.039
And  I'm  honestly  surprised  about  the
quality  of  the  code  that  I'm  receiving.

00:34:19.300 --> 00:34:25.539
And  this  might  be  possible  because  I  wouldn't
be  able  to  recreate  it  from  scratch  by  myself.

00:34:26.699 --> 00:34:32.280
I'm  pretty  sure  that  this  is  something
that  other  people  feel  differently  about.

00:34:34.179 --> 00:34:37.900
At  this  point,  thank  you  so
much  for  attending  this  talk.

00:34:39.320 --> 00:34:44.000
I  feel  obligated  on  wishing  you  a
good  night  after  the  Q &A  session.

00:34:44.179 --> 00:34:48.440
And  also,  don't  forget
the  1-2-8  rule  because

00:34:48.440 --> 00:34:51.000
there  are  plenty  of  more
interesting  talks  tomorrow

00:34:51.000 --> 00:34:54.320
and  you  have  to  be  in  good  shape  for
that.  Thank  you  so  much.  *applause*

00:34:54.320 --> 00:35:18.159
Yeah,  thanks  for  the  talk.  We  have  time
for  one  question  there  from  the  internet.

00:35:18.679 --> 00:35:19.659
So,  the  signal  angel.

00:35:21.440 --> 00:35:26.280
Signal: Okay,  there  is  a  question  on  how
can  you  identify  AI -generated  content

00:35:26.280 --> 00:35:29.719
when  it  doesn't  have  any  obvious
errors  like  you  mentioned?

00:35:30.119 --> 00:35:33.940
And  doesn't  it  have  a  risk
to  flag  false  positives?

00:35:33.940 --> 00:35:34.880
Mathias: Yeah.

00:35:38.460 --> 00:35:43.300
At  this  point,  I  don't
feel  qualified  to  identify,

00:35:43.679 --> 00:35:47.099
to  positively  identify
LLM -generated  content.

00:35:47.260 --> 00:35:54.380
There  are  some  telltale  signs  about  the
use  of  adjectives,  certain  structures.

00:35:54.880 --> 00:36:02.519
As  I  said,  there  are  syntax  and  template
issues.  This  is  just  a  suspicion.

00:36:02.519 --> 00:36:11.579
And  there  are  a  couple  of  other  reasons
why  you  have  to  be  careful  about  this.

00:36:11.860 --> 00:36:19.480
And  also,  there  is  a  general  reluctance  of
admitting  the  use  of  LLMs  for  many  reasons.

00:36:20.940 --> 00:36:23.860
At  a  certain  point,  it's  up
to  the  community  to  decide

00:36:23.860 --> 00:36:28.179
whether  the  suspicion al one
should  be  reason  enough

00:36:28.179 --> 00:36:36.719
to  quarantine  the  content  and  to
see  what  else  should  be  done.

00:36:38.059 --> 00:36:43.820
But  in  many  cases,  you
can  work  backwards  and  say,

00:36:43.880 --> 00:36:47.739
okay,  I  found  a  person
publishing  something  that

00:36:47.739 --> 00:36:51.179
is  clearly  AI -generated
for  a  couple  of  reasons,

00:36:51.179 --> 00:36:52.780
and  then  I  can  go  backwards.

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:55.739
And  the  community  consensus
in  Wikipedia  seems  to  be

00:36:55.739 --> 00:37:00.780
that  if  the  person  is  unable
to  provide  specific  evidence

00:37:00.780 --> 00:37:08.519
on  the  starting  point  of  when  the  AI  use
started,  the  cutoff  date  is  simply  November  2022.

00:37:08.519 --> 00:37:13.940
And  we  have  to  delete  the  things  that
have  been  uploaded  after  November  2022.

00:37:14.519 --> 00:37:21.320
It  might  sound  harsh,  but  I
don't  think  that  Wikipedians  are

00:37:21.320 --> 00:37:25.460
able  to  scale  to  the  extent
that  LLM  models  do  right  now.

00:37:26.500 --> 00:37:30.659
Herald: And  thank  you  again  for  this  great  talk,
and  I  ask  of  you  a  great  round  of  applause.
